1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,662
Newton's laws of motion are the
foundation of classical mechanics.

2
00:00:04,962 --> 00:00:09,704
In this lesson, we'll build them
up carefully, one law at a time.

3
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:14,627
Why does a hockey puck
glide so far across the ice,

4
00:00:14,627 --> 00:00:18,793
while a book pushed across a
table stops almost at once?

5
00:00:19,093 --> 00:00:22,464
How can a rocket speed up
in the emptiness of space,

6
00:00:22,464 --> 00:00:24,992
where there is nothing to push against?

7
00:00:25,292 --> 00:00:29,812
And why do you feel heavier in a
lift that is just starting to go up?

8
00:00:30,233 --> 00:00:35,109
All three questions have the
same answer: three short laws,

9
00:00:35,109 --> 00:00:40,729
published by Isaac Newton in 1687,
in his book called the Principia.

10
00:00:41,029 --> 00:00:44,008
They describe how forces change the motion

11
00:00:44,008 --> 00:00:47,128
of everything from
cricket balls to planets.

12
00:00:48,167 --> 00:00:49,362
Here is our plan.

13
00:00:49,662 --> 00:00:52,112
We start with the key idea of inertia.

14
00:00:52,412 --> 00:00:54,411
Then we take each law in turn,

15
00:00:54,411 --> 00:00:57,409
and see how the second
and third laws lead to

16
00:00:57,409 --> 00:01:00,341
impulse and to the
conservation of momentum.

17
00:01:00,641 --> 00:01:06,649
Next, we learn a step-by-step method for
solving problems with free-body diagrams,

18
00:01:06,649 --> 00:01:10,019
and we finish with a
summary and a short quiz.

19
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:16,514
For nearly two thousand years,

20
00:01:16,514 --> 00:01:21,459
most people accepted the ideas of
the Greek philosopher Aristotle.

21
00:01:21,759 --> 00:01:26,387
He taught that a moving object needs
a continuous push to keep it moving.

22
00:01:26,687 --> 00:01:28,839
Stop pushing, and it stops.

23
00:01:29,267 --> 00:01:33,625
That seems sensible, because
it matches everyday experience.

24
00:01:33,925 --> 00:01:36,376
But in the early sixteen-hundreds,

25
00:01:36,376 --> 00:01:39,980
Galileo Galilei noticed
what Aristotle had missed.

26
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:42,750
The book does not stop by itself.

27
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:45,723
It stops because a force acts on it:

28
00:01:45,723 --> 00:01:49,436
friction from the table,
which opposes its motion.

29
00:01:49,867 --> 00:01:50,745
Watch again.

30
00:01:51,045 --> 00:01:54,218
While the push and the
friction are equal in size,

31
00:01:54,218 --> 00:01:56,375
the book slides at a steady speed.

32
00:01:56,675 --> 00:02:01,468
Once the push stops, friction is
the only horizontal force left,

33
00:02:01,468 --> 00:02:03,490
and it slows the book down.

34
00:02:04,533 --> 00:02:07,817
The smoother the surface,
the weaker the friction,

35
00:02:07,817 --> 00:02:09,985
and the farther an object slides.

36
00:02:10,285 --> 00:02:14,402
Here, three identical blocks
start with the same speed:

37
00:02:14,402 --> 00:02:17,921
on rough wood, on a
polished floor, and on ice.

38
00:02:18,367 --> 00:02:22,404
On ice, the friction is so weak
that the block is still sliding,

39
00:02:22,404 --> 00:02:24,800
long after the other two have stopped.

40
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:27,804
So what would happen
with no friction at all?

41
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:32,275
Galileo answered with a
brilliant thought experiment.

42
00:02:32,575 --> 00:02:36,778
Let a ball roll down a ramp,
and up a second ramp facing it.

43
00:02:37,078 --> 00:02:39,650
If no energy is lost along the way,

44
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:43,984
the ball climbs back to exactly
the height it started from.

45
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:47,144
Now make the second ramp less steep.

46
00:02:47,444 --> 00:02:50,616
To reach the same height,
the ball has to roll farther.

47
00:02:51,633 --> 00:02:55,253
Make it gentler still, and
the ball rolls farther again.

48
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:04,265
And if the second ramp is perfectly flat?

49
00:03:04,565 --> 00:03:09,213
The ball can never get back to its
starting height, so it never stops.

50
00:03:09,513 --> 00:03:14,298
It keeps rolling in a straight
line, at constant speed, forever.

51
00:03:15,967 --> 00:03:18,373
This was Galileo's great insight.

52
00:03:18,673 --> 00:03:21,747
An object does not need
a force to keep moving.

53
00:03:22,047 --> 00:03:25,251
A force is needed only
to change its motion:

54
00:03:25,251 --> 00:03:29,622
to speed it up, to slow it down,
or to change its direction.

55
00:03:29,922 --> 00:03:33,114
Newton made this idea the
first of his three laws.

56
00:03:37,433 --> 00:03:40,918
Newton's first law says:
a body stays at rest,

57
00:03:40,918 --> 00:03:44,932
or keeps moving in a straight
line at constant speed,

58
00:03:44,932 --> 00:03:47,886
unless a net external force acts on it.

59
00:03:48,186 --> 00:03:54,116
In symbols: if the net force on a body
is zero, its velocity is constant.

60
00:03:54,416 --> 00:03:59,244
And if its velocity is constant,
the net force on it must be zero.

61
00:04:00,267 --> 00:04:03,618
Pay attention to the
words: in a straight line.

62
00:04:03,918 --> 00:04:08,529
Constant velocity means constant
speed and constant direction.

63
00:04:08,829 --> 00:04:12,503
A car going round a curve
at a steady 50 kilometres

64
00:04:12,503 --> 00:04:15,458
per hour does not have constant velocity,

65
00:04:15,458 --> 00:04:18,124
because its direction keeps changing.

66
00:04:18,424 --> 00:04:20,520
So a net force must act on it.

67
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:24,558
Here, it's the sideways friction
of the road on the tyres,

68
00:04:24,558 --> 00:04:27,200
pointing towards the centre of the curve.

69
00:04:28,267 --> 00:04:30,936
Now pay attention to the word: net.

70
00:04:31,236 --> 00:04:34,326
Many forces can act on a body at once.

71
00:04:34,626 --> 00:04:38,318
If they balance, so that
their vector sum is zero,

72
00:04:38,318 --> 00:04:42,527
the body moves exactly as if
no force acted on it at all.

73
00:04:42,827 --> 00:04:46,722
A book lying on a table
feels two forces: its weight,

74
00:04:46,722 --> 00:04:51,279
pulling down, and the normal
force from the table, pushing up.

75
00:04:51,579 --> 00:04:54,532
They balance, so the book stays at rest.

76
00:04:55,567 --> 00:05:00,263
A car cruising at a steady speed on
a straight, level road is the same.

77
00:05:00,563 --> 00:05:05,171
The road pushes the car forward,
through friction on its driven wheels,

78
00:05:05,171 --> 00:05:09,130
while air resistance and rolling
resistance push it backward.

79
00:05:09,430 --> 00:05:15,430
When these forces balance, the net force
is zero, and the velocity stays constant.

80
00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:20,938
With no resistive forces at all, the car
would cruise on with no engine force.

81
00:05:21,238 --> 00:05:25,249
The engine is needed only to
cancel the resistive forces.

82
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:29,391
The first law is also
called the law of inertia.

83
00:05:29,691 --> 00:05:34,506
Inertia is the tendency of a body
to resist any change in its motion.

84
00:05:34,806 --> 00:05:36,888
And mass is the measure of inertia.

85
00:05:37,188 --> 00:05:42,451
Give an empty trolley and a heavily
loaded one exactly the same short push.

86
00:05:42,751 --> 00:05:47,012
The empty one shoots off, while
the loaded one barely moves.

87
00:05:47,312 --> 00:05:51,106
The more mass a body has, the
harder it is to speed it up,

88
00:05:51,106 --> 00:05:53,133
to slow it down, or to turn it.

89
00:05:54,167 --> 00:05:57,034
Here's a demonstration
you can try at home.

90
00:05:57,334 --> 00:06:00,764
Put a card on top of a glass,
and a coin on the card.

91
00:06:01,064 --> 00:06:02,850
Flick the card away sharply.

92
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:08,840
The card flies off, but the coin's inertia
keeps it almost exactly where it was,

93
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:11,472
and it drops straight into the glass.

94
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:16,013
You feel your own inertia
whenever you ride in a bus.

95
00:06:16,313 --> 00:06:20,863
When the bus starts suddenly, the
floor carries your feet forward,

96
00:06:20,863 --> 00:06:25,688
but your upper body tends to stay
where it was, so you lurch backward.

97
00:06:25,988 --> 00:06:30,830
When the bus brakes suddenly, your
body tends to keep moving forward.

98
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:34,181
That is exactly why seat belts save lives.

99
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:37,493
The bus teaches us something deeper.

100
00:06:37,793 --> 00:06:40,875
Suppose a ball rests on
the floor of the bus,

101
00:06:40,875 --> 00:06:45,396
which is so smooth that it exerts
no horizontal force on the ball.

102
00:06:45,696 --> 00:06:47,282
Now the bus speeds up.

103
00:06:47,582 --> 00:06:52,098
Seen from the roadside, nothing
pushes the ball horizontally,

104
00:06:52,098 --> 00:06:56,613
so it stays exactly where it
was, just as the first law says.

105
00:06:56,913 --> 00:06:58,893
But seen from inside the bus,

106
00:06:58,893 --> 00:07:03,673
the ball suddenly starts moving
backward, although no force pushes it.

107
00:07:03,973 --> 00:07:08,016
For someone riding in the bus,
the first law seems to fail.

108
00:07:09,033 --> 00:07:14,274
Frames of reference in which the first
law holds are called inertial frames.

109
00:07:14,574 --> 00:07:19,150
The ground is, to a very good
approximation, an inertial frame,

110
00:07:19,150 --> 00:07:23,726
and so is any frame moving at
constant velocity relative to it.

111
00:07:24,026 --> 00:07:27,254
An accelerating bus is
a non-inertial frame.

112
00:07:27,554 --> 00:07:32,540
Newton's laws, in the form we
use, hold only in inertial frames.

113
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:37,681
So the first law does more than
describe what happens with zero force:

114
00:07:37,681 --> 00:07:41,278
it tells us which frames
the laws of motion work in.

115
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:46,012
So, the first law in one
line: zero net force,

116
00:07:46,012 --> 00:07:51,014
if and only if the velocity is
constant, in an inertial frame.

117
00:07:51,314 --> 00:07:54,050
And the greater the mass,
the greater the inertia.

118
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:02,431
The first law tells us what
happens when the net force is zero.

119
00:08:02,731 --> 00:08:05,994
The second law tells us
what happens when it is not.

120
00:08:06,294 --> 00:08:10,714
To state it, Newton used a quantity
he called the quantity of motion.

121
00:08:11,014 --> 00:08:12,960
Today we call it momentum.

122
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:15,969
Momentum is mass times velocity.

123
00:08:16,269 --> 00:08:19,985
It is a vector, pointing the
same way as the velocity,

124
00:08:19,985 --> 00:08:23,357
and it is measured in
kilogram metres per second.

125
00:08:23,657 --> 00:08:28,836
A loaded truck is much harder to stop
than a bicycle moving at the same speed,

126
00:08:28,836 --> 00:08:32,687
and a fast cricket ball is
harder to stop than a slow one.

127
00:08:32,987 --> 00:08:35,612
Momentum captures both effects.

128
00:08:36,633 --> 00:08:40,835
Newton's second law states: the
rate of change of momentum of

129
00:08:40,835 --> 00:08:45,106
a body is proportional to the
net external force acting on it,

130
00:08:45,106 --> 00:08:48,343
and takes place in the
direction of that force.

131
00:08:48,643 --> 00:08:53,572
In SI units, the constant of
proportionality is exactly one.

132
00:08:53,872 --> 00:08:57,254
So, the net force equals dp/dt.

133
00:08:57,700 --> 00:09:02,729
If the mass of the body stays constant,
the mass comes outside the derivative.

134
00:09:03,029 --> 00:09:07,121
The rate of change of m v is m
times the rate of change of v,

135
00:09:07,121 --> 00:09:10,810
and the rate of change of
velocity is the acceleration.

136
00:09:11,110 --> 00:09:16,503
So, for constant mass, the net force
equals mass times acceleration.

137
00:09:16,803 --> 00:09:19,371
This is the form you will use most often.

138
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:25,857
Rearranged, the acceleration equals
the net force divided by the mass.

139
00:09:26,157 --> 00:09:29,778
So the acceleration is
proportional to the net force,

140
00:09:29,778 --> 00:09:32,170
inversely proportional to the mass,

141
00:09:32,170 --> 00:09:35,928
and it always points in the
direction of the net force,

142
00:09:35,928 --> 00:09:39,275
which is not necessarily
the direction of motion.

143
00:09:39,575 --> 00:09:43,412
And if the net force is zero,
the acceleration is zero,

144
00:09:43,412 --> 00:09:45,644
which agrees with the first law.

145
00:09:46,667 --> 00:09:48,272
Let's see this in action.

146
00:09:48,572 --> 00:09:54,027
Two identical one kilogram carts start
from rest on a frictionless track.

147
00:09:54,327 --> 00:09:57,669
The top one is pulled with
a net force of one newton,

148
00:09:57,669 --> 00:09:59,686
the bottom one with two newtons.

149
00:09:59,986 --> 00:10:03,385
Double the force gives
double the acceleration:

150
00:10:03,385 --> 00:10:06,856
one metre per second
squared, compared with two.

151
00:10:07,156 --> 00:10:11,467
So in the same time, the bottom
cart travels twice as far.

152
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:16,429
Now keep the force fixed at two
newtons, but double the mass.

153
00:10:16,729 --> 00:10:19,966
The two kilogram cart accelerates at only

154
00:10:19,966 --> 00:10:23,361
one metre per second
squared: half as much.

155
00:10:23,661 --> 00:10:26,524
Doubling the inertia
halves the acceleration.

156
00:10:27,567 --> 00:10:30,337
This also defines the unit of force.

157
00:10:30,637 --> 00:10:34,264
One newton is the net force
that gives a mass of one

158
00:10:34,264 --> 00:10:38,240
kilogram an acceleration of
one metre per second squared.

159
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:43,204
So one newton equals one kilogram
metre per second squared.

160
00:10:44,233 --> 00:10:46,885
Force and acceleration are vectors,

161
00:10:46,885 --> 00:10:50,748
so the second law holds
separately along each axis:

162
00:10:50,748 --> 00:10:54,990
the sum of the x components
of the forces equals m times

163
00:10:54,990 --> 00:10:59,232
the x component of the
acceleration, and the same for y.

164
00:10:59,532 --> 00:11:01,845
A thrown ball shows this beautifully.

165
00:11:02,267 --> 00:11:05,960
Once it leaves your hand,
if we ignore air resistance,

166
00:11:05,960 --> 00:11:09,380
the only force on it is
its weight, straight down.

167
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:12,061
Horizontally there is no force,

168
00:11:12,061 --> 00:11:17,591
so the horizontal velocity stays
constant: that's the first law at work.

169
00:11:17,891 --> 00:11:22,585
Vertically, the weight gives a
constant downward acceleration, g.

170
00:11:22,885 --> 00:11:26,113
Together, they make the
curved path of a projectile.

171
00:11:27,133 --> 00:11:28,941
One more important point.

172
00:11:29,241 --> 00:11:34,095
The second law links the net force to
the acceleration at the same instant.

173
00:11:34,395 --> 00:11:36,517
If the force suddenly stops,

174
00:11:36,517 --> 00:11:41,518
the acceleration drops to zero at
once, but the velocity does not.

175
00:11:41,818 --> 00:11:45,484
The body simply carries on
with the velocity it had,

176
00:11:45,484 --> 00:11:47,387
just as the first law says.

177
00:11:48,433 --> 00:11:50,847
So, the second law in one line:

178
00:11:50,847 --> 00:11:54,897
the net force equals the
rate of change of momentum,

179
00:11:54,897 --> 00:11:59,024
which, for constant mass,
is mass times acceleration.

180
00:11:59,324 --> 00:12:03,305
And the acceleration always
points along the net force.

181
00:12:07,633 --> 00:12:12,032
The momentum form of the second
law leads to a very useful idea.

182
00:12:12,332 --> 00:12:16,926
If a constant net force acts
on a body for a time delta t,

183
00:12:16,926 --> 00:12:20,729
the momentum changes by
the force times delta t.

184
00:12:21,167 --> 00:12:24,572
If the force varies, as
it does in any collision,

185
00:12:24,572 --> 00:12:29,716
the change in momentum equals the area
under the force against time graph.

186
00:12:30,016 --> 00:12:34,159
We can also write it as the
average force times delta t.

187
00:12:34,459 --> 00:12:38,886
This quantity is called impulse,
and its unit is the newton second,

188
00:12:38,886 --> 00:12:42,123
which is the same as a
kilogram metre per second.

189
00:12:43,167 --> 00:12:44,596
Here's why it matters.

190
00:12:44,896 --> 00:12:49,860
A fielder catches a cricket
ball of mass 0.16 kilograms,

191
00:12:49,860 --> 00:12:55,001
travelling at 25 metres per
second, and brings it to rest.

192
00:12:55,301 --> 00:12:59,453
The ball's change in
momentum is 0.16 times 25,

193
00:12:59,453 --> 00:13:05,285
which is 4 kilogram metres per
second, however the ball is caught.

194
00:13:05,733 --> 00:13:08,112
If the fielder keeps her hands stiff,

195
00:13:08,112 --> 00:13:11,198
the ball stops in about
a hundredth of a second.

196
00:13:11,498 --> 00:13:17,935
The average force is then 4 divided
by 0.01, which is 400 newtons.

197
00:13:18,235 --> 00:13:19,091
That stings!

198
00:13:19,391 --> 00:13:22,682
But if she draws her
hands back as she catches,

199
00:13:22,682 --> 00:13:25,762
and stops the ball over
a tenth of a second,

200
00:13:25,762 --> 00:13:28,353
the average force is only 40 newtons.

201
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:34,786
Same change in momentum, ten times
the time, one tenth of the force.

202
00:13:35,086 --> 00:13:38,957
The same idea explains crumple
zones and airbags in cars,

203
00:13:38,957 --> 00:13:41,606
the thick soft mats in a high-jump pit,

204
00:13:41,606 --> 00:13:45,273
and why you bend your knees
when you land from a jump.

205
00:13:45,573 --> 00:13:50,889
In every case, a longer stopping
time means a smaller average force.

206
00:13:55,233 --> 00:14:00,789
Newton's third law says: whenever one
body exerts a force on a second body,

207
00:14:00,789 --> 00:14:05,086
the second body simultaneously
exerts a force on the first

208
00:14:05,086 --> 00:14:09,013
that is equal in magnitude
and opposite in direction.

209
00:14:09,313 --> 00:14:15,035
You may have heard it as: to every action,
there is an equal and opposite reaction.

210
00:14:16,067 --> 00:14:18,725
So a force is never a one-sided thing.

211
00:14:19,025 --> 00:14:24,358
It is always an interaction between two
bodies, and it always comes as a pair.

212
00:14:24,658 --> 00:14:29,305
Push on a wall, and the wall pushes
back on your hand, just as hard.

213
00:14:30,333 --> 00:14:33,693
Four facts about these
pairs are worth remembering.

214
00:14:33,993 --> 00:14:37,698
One: the two forces act
on different bodies.

215
00:14:37,998 --> 00:14:42,603
Two: they are always equal in
size and opposite in direction,

216
00:14:42,603 --> 00:14:47,434
whatever the bodies are doing:
at rest, moving, or accelerating.

217
00:14:47,734 --> 00:14:50,213
Three: they act at the same instant.

218
00:14:50,513 --> 00:14:55,893
Neither one causes the other, so the
words action and reaction are only labels.

219
00:14:56,193 --> 00:14:58,992
Four: they are the same kind of force.

220
00:14:59,292 --> 00:15:02,429
If one is gravitational, so is the other.

221
00:15:02,729 --> 00:15:06,051
If one is a contact
force, so is the other.

222
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:12,072
Because the two forces act on different
bodies, they can never cancel each other.

223
00:15:12,372 --> 00:15:15,972
Forces cancel only when
they act on the same body.

224
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,701
Watch two skaters, standing
still on smooth ice.

225
00:15:21,001 --> 00:15:28,019
Anna, with a mass of 50 kilograms, pushes
Ben, who has a mass of 75 kilograms.

226
00:15:28,319 --> 00:15:30,533
At every instant during the push,

227
00:15:30,533 --> 00:15:34,691
the force on Ben and the force
on Anna are equal and opposite,

228
00:15:34,691 --> 00:15:38,313
so both of them start to
move, in opposite directions.

229
00:15:38,733 --> 00:15:41,990
But equal forces don't mean equal effects.

230
00:15:42,290 --> 00:15:48,524
With a 750 newton push, Anna accelerates
at 15 metres per second squared,

231
00:15:48,524 --> 00:15:52,794
while Ben, who is heavier,
accelerates at only 10.

232
00:15:53,094 --> 00:15:56,847
So after the push, which
lasts a fifth of a second,

233
00:15:56,847 --> 00:16:00,820
Anna glides away at 3 metres
per second, and Ben at 2.

234
00:16:01,867 --> 00:16:07,498
The third law explains how people, animals
and vehicles set themselves in motion.

235
00:16:07,798 --> 00:16:11,430
When you walk, your foot
pushes backward on the ground,

236
00:16:11,430 --> 00:16:13,873
and the ground pushes forward on you.

237
00:16:14,173 --> 00:16:18,580
That forward push, a friction
force, is what accelerates you,

238
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:21,397
which is why walking on ice is so hard.

239
00:16:21,833 --> 00:16:23,773
A rocket works the same way.

240
00:16:24,073 --> 00:16:27,652
It pushes its exhaust gases
backward at high speed,

241
00:16:27,652 --> 00:16:30,320
and the gases push the rocket forward.

242
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:33,365
A rocket doesn't need air to push against,

243
00:16:33,365 --> 00:16:36,306
which is why it works
in the vacuum of space.

244
00:16:37,333 --> 00:16:39,782
Even gravity obeys the third law.

245
00:16:40,082 --> 00:16:46,339
The Earth pulls a 0.1 kilogram apple
down with a force of about 0.98 newtons,

246
00:16:46,339 --> 00:16:51,296
and the apple pulls the Earth
up with exactly the same force.

247
00:16:51,596 --> 00:16:56,279
The apple accelerates at 9.8
metres per second squared.

248
00:16:56,579 --> 00:17:03,048
But the Earth, with its enormous mass,
accelerates by only about 1.6 times ten to

249
00:17:03,048 --> 00:17:09,277
the minus twenty-five metres per second
squared: far too small ever to notice.

250
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:12,246
Now, a classic trap.

251
00:17:12,546 --> 00:17:14,301
A book rests on a table.

252
00:17:14,601 --> 00:17:18,392
Two forces act on the book:
its weight, pulling down,

253
00:17:18,392 --> 00:17:21,825
and the normal force from
the table, pushing up.

254
00:17:22,125 --> 00:17:23,592
They're equal and opposite.

255
00:17:23,892 --> 00:17:26,511
So, are they an action–reaction pair?

256
00:17:26,811 --> 00:17:27,840
Pause and think.

257
00:17:31,667 --> 00:17:32,946
No, they are not.

258
00:17:33,246 --> 00:17:36,550
Both forces act on the
same body, the book,

259
00:17:36,550 --> 00:17:39,470
and they are different kinds of force:

260
00:17:39,470 --> 00:17:43,389
one is gravitational, the
other is a contact force.

261
00:17:43,689 --> 00:17:47,402
They're equal here only because
the book isn't accelerating.

262
00:17:47,702 --> 00:17:50,550
That's the first law
at work, not the third.

263
00:17:51,467 --> 00:17:54,049
The true partner of the book's weight is

264
00:17:54,049 --> 00:17:56,826
the book's gravitational
pull on the Earth,

265
00:17:56,826 --> 00:17:59,215
acting on the Earth, and pointing up.

266
00:17:59,515 --> 00:18:04,135
And the partner of the normal force is
the push of the book down on the table.

267
00:18:04,435 --> 00:18:06,483
Here are the two genuine pairs.

268
00:18:07,500 --> 00:18:08,951
Here's one more puzzle.

269
00:18:09,251 --> 00:18:10,744
A horse pulls a cart.

270
00:18:11,044 --> 00:18:15,336
By the third law, the cart pulls
back on the horse just as hard.

271
00:18:15,636 --> 00:18:17,962
So how can they ever start moving?

272
00:18:18,262 --> 00:18:20,469
Pause the video and think about it.

273
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:28,976
The key is to look at the
forces on each body separately.

274
00:18:29,276 --> 00:18:32,240
On the cart, there's the
horse's forward pull,

275
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:35,140
and friction from the
ground holding it back.

276
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:38,970
If the pull is bigger, the
cart accelerates forward.

277
00:18:39,270 --> 00:18:42,309
On the horse, there's
the cart's backward pull,

278
00:18:42,309 --> 00:18:45,478
and the forward push of
the ground on its hooves,

279
00:18:45,478 --> 00:18:48,582
because the horse pushes
backward on the ground.

280
00:18:48,882 --> 00:18:53,227
If the ground's push is bigger,
the horse accelerates forward too.

281
00:18:53,527 --> 00:18:56,873
The pair of forces between
horse and cart acts

282
00:18:56,873 --> 00:19:00,145
on two different bodies,
so it never cancels.

283
00:19:01,167 --> 00:19:06,020
So, the third law in one line:
the force on body one due to body

284
00:19:06,020 --> 00:19:11,101
two is equal and opposite to the
force on body two due to body one.

285
00:19:11,401 --> 00:19:14,134
The two forces act at the same instant,

286
00:19:14,134 --> 00:19:17,638
are of the same kind, and
act on different bodies.

287
00:19:21,967 --> 00:19:26,886
Put the second and third laws together,
and something remarkable follows.

288
00:19:27,186 --> 00:19:30,615
Take two bodies that interact
only with each other.

289
00:19:30,915 --> 00:19:33,439
By the third law, at every instant,

290
00:19:33,439 --> 00:19:37,044
the force on body one due
to body two is equal and

291
00:19:37,044 --> 00:19:40,650
opposite to the force on
body two due to body one.

292
00:19:40,950 --> 00:19:44,047
By the second law, each
force equals the rate

293
00:19:44,047 --> 00:19:47,145
of change of momentum
of the body it acts on.

294
00:19:47,567 --> 00:19:48,274
Now add them.

295
00:19:48,574 --> 00:19:51,434
The rate of change of
the total momentum is

296
00:19:51,434 --> 00:19:54,161
the sum of the two forces, which is zero.

297
00:19:54,461 --> 00:19:57,348
So the total momentum never changes.

298
00:19:58,567 --> 00:20:02,089
This is the law of conservation
of linear momentum.

299
00:20:02,389 --> 00:20:08,624
If no net external force acts on a
system, its total momentum stays constant,

300
00:20:08,624 --> 00:20:13,645
whatever happens inside it:
collisions, explosions, or pushes.

301
00:20:14,667 --> 00:20:16,874
Let's check this with our skaters.

302
00:20:17,174 --> 00:20:21,971
Before the push, both are at rest,
so the total momentum is zero.

303
00:20:22,271 --> 00:20:26,336
After the push, Anna moves
left at 3 metres per second.

304
00:20:26,636 --> 00:20:32,211
Taking right as positive, her
momentum is 50 times minus 3:

305
00:20:32,211 --> 00:20:35,707
minus 150 kilogram metres per second.

306
00:20:36,007 --> 00:20:43,367
Ben moves right at 2 metres per
second: 75 times 2, which is plus 150.

307
00:20:43,667 --> 00:20:46,127
The total is still exactly zero.

308
00:20:46,567 --> 00:20:48,742
The lighter skater moves faster,

309
00:20:48,742 --> 00:20:52,754
but the two momenta are equal
and opposite, so they cancel.

310
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:57,022
The same reasoning explains
the recoil of a gun.

311
00:20:57,322 --> 00:21:00,288
The bullet gets a large momentum forward,

312
00:21:00,288 --> 00:21:03,398
so the gun gets an
equal momentum backward.

313
00:21:03,698 --> 00:21:07,190
Because the gun is much
heavier, it recoils slowly.

314
00:21:07,633 --> 00:21:10,782
And it's how a rocket keeps accelerating:

315
00:21:10,782 --> 00:21:16,925
the exhaust gases carry momentum backward,
so the rocket gains momentum forward.

316
00:21:21,267 --> 00:21:23,359
Now let's put the laws to work.

317
00:21:23,659 --> 00:21:27,092
Almost every problem
about forces and motion

318
00:21:27,092 --> 00:21:30,135
can be solved with the same five steps.

319
00:21:30,435 --> 00:21:33,400
One: choose the body you want to study.

320
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:36,197
Two: draw its free-body diagram,

321
00:21:36,197 --> 00:21:41,425
showing every external force acting
on that body, and nothing else.

322
00:21:41,725 --> 00:21:47,740
Three: choose axes, ideally with one axis
along the direction of the acceleration.

323
00:21:48,040 --> 00:21:51,915
Four: apply Newton's
second law along each axis.

324
00:21:52,215 --> 00:21:56,397
Five: solve, and check that
your answer makes sense.

325
00:21:57,433 --> 00:22:00,184
These are the forces you
will meet most often.

326
00:22:00,484 --> 00:22:03,070
Weight, m g, acting straight down.

327
00:22:03,370 --> 00:22:07,720
The normal force, perpendicular
to a surface in contact.

328
00:22:08,020 --> 00:22:10,980
Tension, pulling along a string or rope.

329
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:14,696
Friction, along a
surface, opposing sliding.

330
00:22:14,996 --> 00:22:17,123
And any applied push or pull.

331
00:22:18,167 --> 00:22:19,439
Example one.

332
00:22:19,739 --> 00:22:24,218
A 5 kilogram box rests on a
smooth, frictionless floor.

333
00:22:24,518 --> 00:22:27,591
A rope pulls it with
a force of 20 newtons,

334
00:22:27,591 --> 00:22:30,092
at 30 degrees above the horizontal.

335
00:22:30,392 --> 00:22:34,914
Find the acceleration of the box,
and the normal force from the floor.

336
00:22:35,214 --> 00:22:39,018
Take g as 9.8 metres per second squared.

337
00:22:39,467 --> 00:22:42,183
Step one: the body is the box.

338
00:22:42,483 --> 00:22:45,185
Step two, the free-body diagram.

339
00:22:45,485 --> 00:22:51,303
Its weight is 5 times 9.8, which
is 49 newtons, straight down.

340
00:22:51,603 --> 00:22:56,086
The floor pushes up with a normal
force, N, which we don't know yet.

341
00:22:56,386 --> 00:23:00,081
And the rope pulls with
20 newtons, at 30 degrees.

342
00:23:00,500 --> 00:23:03,057
Step three: take x along the floor,

343
00:23:03,057 --> 00:23:08,903
because that's the direction in which the
box can accelerate, and y straight up.

344
00:23:09,203 --> 00:23:12,502
Then split the pull into components:

345
00:23:12,502 --> 00:23:17,450
20 times the cosine of 30
degrees, about 17.3 newtons,

346
00:23:17,450 --> 00:23:24,323
along x; and 20 times the sine of 30
degrees, which is 10 newtons, along y.

347
00:23:24,767 --> 00:23:25,587
Step four.

348
00:23:25,887 --> 00:23:30,265
Along x, the only force is 17.3 newtons,

349
00:23:30,265 --> 00:23:38,912
so the acceleration is 17.3 divided by
5: about 3.46 metres per second squared.

350
00:23:39,212 --> 00:23:45,304
Along y, the box does not accelerate,
because it stays on the floor,

351
00:23:45,304 --> 00:23:50,320
so the forces balance: N plus
10, minus 49, equals zero.

352
00:23:50,620 --> 00:23:52,727
So N is 39 newtons.

353
00:23:53,167 --> 00:23:54,950
Step five: check.

354
00:23:55,250 --> 00:24:00,190
The normal force is 39 newtons,
less than the weight of 49 newtons,

355
00:24:00,190 --> 00:24:04,319
because the rope is already
supporting 10 newtons of it.

356
00:24:04,619 --> 00:24:09,342
So remember: the normal force
is not always equal to m g.

357
00:24:09,642 --> 00:24:12,795
It is whatever the
surface needs to supply.

358
00:24:14,433 --> 00:24:16,521
Example two: the lift.

359
00:24:16,821 --> 00:24:21,878
A 60 kilogram student stands on
a bathroom scale inside a lift.

360
00:24:22,178 --> 00:24:27,192
A scale measures how hard it pushes
up on you, which is the normal force.

361
00:24:27,492 --> 00:24:31,486
By the third law, that's also
how hard you push down on it.

362
00:24:31,786 --> 00:24:36,672
So, what does the scale read when
the lift moves at constant velocity,

363
00:24:36,672 --> 00:24:40,720
when it accelerates upward at
2 metres per second squared,

364
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:44,908
when it accelerates downward
at 2 metres per second squared,

365
00:24:44,908 --> 00:24:46,653
and when it falls freely?

366
00:24:47,100 --> 00:24:52,086
The free-body diagram of the
student has just two forces:

367
00:24:52,086 --> 00:24:55,060
the normal force N from the scale,

368
00:24:55,060 --> 00:24:57,947
pointing up, and the weight, m g,

369
00:24:57,947 --> 00:25:02,584
which is 60 times 9.8, or
588 newtons, pointing down.

370
00:25:02,884 --> 00:25:06,260
Taking up as positive,
the second law says:

371
00:25:06,260 --> 00:25:10,264
N minus m g equals the mass
times the acceleration.

372
00:25:10,564 --> 00:25:14,889
So N equals the mass times,
g plus the acceleration.

373
00:25:16,633 --> 00:25:23,357
At constant velocity, the acceleration
is zero, so N is 588 newtons.

374
00:25:23,657 --> 00:25:27,147
The scale shows your true weight,
even though you are moving.

375
00:25:27,967 --> 00:25:32,840
Accelerating upward at 2
metres per second squared,

376
00:25:32,840 --> 00:25:37,139
N equals 60 times 11.8,
which is 708 newtons.

377
00:25:37,439 --> 00:25:38,476
You feel heavier.

378
00:25:39,300 --> 00:25:44,643
Accelerating downward at 2
metres per second squared,

379
00:25:44,643 --> 00:25:49,079
N equals 60 times 7.8,
which is 468 newtons.

380
00:25:49,379 --> 00:25:50,364
You feel lighter.

381
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:58,838
And in free fall, the acceleration is g,
downward, so N equals 60 times zero: zero.

382
00:25:59,138 --> 00:26:02,826
The scale reads nothing,
and you feel weightless,

383
00:26:02,826 --> 00:26:07,042
even though gravity still
pulls on you with 588 newtons.

384
00:26:07,867 --> 00:26:11,806
Notice that what matters is the
direction of the acceleration,

385
00:26:11,806 --> 00:26:13,585
not the direction of motion.

386
00:26:13,885 --> 00:26:19,323
A lift that is moving down, but slowing
to a stop, is accelerating upward,

387
00:26:19,323 --> 00:26:23,219
so you feel heavier, just
as when it starts going up.

388
00:26:24,867 --> 00:26:27,714
Example three: connected bodies.

389
00:26:28,014 --> 00:26:33,079
Two blocks rest on a frictionless
floor, joined by a light string:

390
00:26:33,079 --> 00:26:37,608
a 4 kilogram block in front,
and a 2 kilogram block behind.

391
00:26:37,908 --> 00:26:40,916
A 12 newton force pulls the front block.

392
00:26:41,216 --> 00:26:44,619
Find the acceleration, and
the tension in the string.

393
00:26:45,067 --> 00:26:48,800
First, treat both blocks
together as one system.

394
00:26:49,100 --> 00:26:53,174
The pulls of the string on the
two blocks are internal forces:

395
00:26:53,174 --> 00:26:57,249
they are equal and opposite, so
they cancel within the system.

396
00:26:57,549 --> 00:27:02,219
The only external horizontal
force is the 12 newton pull,

397
00:27:02,219 --> 00:27:05,332
acting on a total mass of 6 kilograms.

398
00:27:05,632 --> 00:27:11,667
So the acceleration is 12 divided
by 6: 2 metres per second squared.

399
00:27:12,900 --> 00:27:17,870
To find the tension, draw a free-body
diagram of the rear block alone.

400
00:27:18,170 --> 00:27:22,036
The only horizontal force
on it is the tension, T.

401
00:27:22,336 --> 00:27:28,754
So T equals 2 kilograms times 2 metres
per second squared, which is 4 newtons.

402
00:27:29,700 --> 00:27:33,546
Check with the front
block: 12 newtons forward,

403
00:27:33,546 --> 00:27:36,492
minus 4 newtons of tension backward,

404
00:27:36,492 --> 00:27:40,993
leaves 8 newtons, which is
exactly 4 kilograms times 2.

405
00:27:41,293 --> 00:27:42,118
It works.

406
00:27:42,418 --> 00:27:45,943
And notice that the tension
is less than 12 newtons,

407
00:27:45,943 --> 00:27:49,807
because the string only has
to accelerate the rear block.

408
00:27:50,233 --> 00:27:54,391
Both blocks share the same
velocity and the same acceleration,

409
00:27:54,391 --> 00:27:57,007
because the string keeps them together.

410
00:28:01,333 --> 00:28:03,038
Let's pull it all together.

411
00:28:03,338 --> 00:28:09,396
First law: if the net force on a body
is zero, its velocity stays constant.

412
00:28:09,696 --> 00:28:13,073
This is inertia, and it
holds in inertial frames.

413
00:28:13,373 --> 00:28:18,243
Second law: the net force equals
the rate of change of momentum,

414
00:28:18,243 --> 00:28:22,277
which, for constant mass,
is mass times acceleration.

415
00:28:22,577 --> 00:28:26,476
Third law: forces come in
equal and opposite pairs,

416
00:28:26,476 --> 00:28:28,540
acting on different bodies.

417
00:28:28,840 --> 00:28:31,732
And from the second and
third laws together,

418
00:28:31,732 --> 00:28:34,952
we get impulse, and the
conservation of momentum.

419
00:28:36,600 --> 00:28:40,283
Before the quiz, let's clear
up four common mistakes.

420
00:28:40,583 --> 00:28:45,155
Mistake one: a moving object
needs a force to keep it moving.

421
00:28:45,455 --> 00:28:49,972
In fact, a force is needed to
change velocity, not to keep it.

422
00:28:50,400 --> 00:28:56,338
Mistake two: heavier objects fall faster,
because gravity pulls them harder.

423
00:28:56,638 --> 00:29:00,297
In fact, the bigger force
acts on a bigger mass,

424
00:29:00,297 --> 00:29:03,194
so the acceleration, m g divided by m,

425
00:29:03,194 --> 00:29:08,683
is the same g for every object, as
long as air resistance is negligible.

426
00:29:09,100 --> 00:29:13,027
Mistake three: action and
reaction cancel each other.

427
00:29:13,327 --> 00:29:17,996
In fact, they act on different
bodies, so they can never cancel.

428
00:29:18,433 --> 00:29:23,317
Mistake four: the net force always
points in the direction of motion.

429
00:29:23,617 --> 00:29:27,414
In fact, it points in the
direction of the acceleration.

430
00:29:27,714 --> 00:29:33,330
A ball thrown upward is moving up, but the
net force on it, its weight, points down.

431
00:29:34,367 --> 00:29:35,795
One last remark.

432
00:29:36,095 --> 00:29:39,867
Newton's laws describe
everything from cricket balls

433
00:29:39,867 --> 00:29:43,277
to planets and spacecraft
with superb accuracy.

434
00:29:43,577 --> 00:29:48,411
They need to be modified only at
speeds close to the speed of light,

435
00:29:48,411 --> 00:29:50,898
where we use Einstein's relativity,

436
00:29:50,898 --> 00:29:55,021
and for atoms and smaller,
where we use quantum mechanics.

437
00:29:56,667 --> 00:29:58,453
Now, test yourself.

438
00:29:58,753 --> 00:29:59,899
Question one.

439
00:30:00,199 --> 00:30:05,560
A space probe, far from any star or
planet, switches off its engines.

440
00:30:05,860 --> 00:30:07,677
What happens to its motion?

441
00:30:07,977 --> 00:30:09,819
Pause the video and decide.

442
00:30:16,067 --> 00:30:20,654
Answer: it keeps moving in a
straight line at constant speed,

443
00:30:20,654 --> 00:30:22,760
until some force acts on it.

444
00:30:23,060 --> 00:30:29,001
It does not slow down and stop: with no
net force, its velocity doesn't change.

445
00:30:30,067 --> 00:30:31,040
Question two.

446
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:33,889
A truck and a mosquito collide head-on.

447
00:30:34,189 --> 00:30:36,407
Which one feels the bigger force?

448
00:30:36,707 --> 00:30:39,498
And which one has the bigger acceleration?

449
00:30:39,798 --> 00:30:40,827
Pause and think.

450
00:30:47,067 --> 00:30:51,439
Answer: the forces are exactly
equal, by the third law.

451
00:30:51,739 --> 00:30:55,743
But the mosquito's mass is
tiny, so, by the second law,

452
00:30:55,743 --> 00:31:01,202
its acceleration is enormous, while
the truck's is far too small to notice.

453
00:31:02,233 --> 00:31:04,770
Question three, a calculation.

454
00:31:05,070 --> 00:31:08,018
Two masses, 3 kilograms and 5 kilograms,

455
00:31:08,018 --> 00:31:13,620
hang from the ends of a light string
that passes over a frictionless pulley.

456
00:31:13,920 --> 00:31:15,888
This is called an Atwood machine.

457
00:31:16,188 --> 00:31:20,334
Find the acceleration of the masses,
and the tension in the string.

458
00:31:20,634 --> 00:31:22,876
Use g equals 9.8.

459
00:31:23,176 --> 00:31:27,333
Pause the video, and work it
out with free-body diagrams.

460
00:31:36,567 --> 00:31:37,788
Here's the solution.

461
00:31:38,088 --> 00:31:43,105
The 5 kilogram mass moves down,
and the 3 kilogram mass moves up,

462
00:31:43,105 --> 00:31:45,883
with accelerations of the same size.

463
00:31:46,333 --> 00:31:50,832
For the 5 kilogram mass,
taking down as positive:

464
00:31:50,832 --> 00:31:55,054
5 g, minus T, equals 5
times the acceleration.

465
00:31:55,354 --> 00:31:59,605
For the 3 kilogram mass,
taking up as positive:

466
00:31:59,605 --> 00:32:03,766
T, minus 3 g, equals 3
times the acceleration.

467
00:32:04,200 --> 00:32:07,965
Add the two equations,
and the tension cancels:

468
00:32:07,965 --> 00:32:10,849
2 g equals 8 times the acceleration.

469
00:32:11,149 --> 00:32:17,881
So the acceleration is g over 4, which
is 2.45 metres per second squared.

470
00:32:18,181 --> 00:32:23,945
Then T equals 3 times,
g plus the acceleration,

471
00:32:23,945 --> 00:32:28,605
which is 3 times 12.25: 36.75 newtons.

472
00:32:29,033 --> 00:32:32,709
Check: the tension lies
between the two weights,

473
00:32:32,709 --> 00:32:35,542
29.4 and 49 newtons, just as it must:

474
00:32:35,542 --> 00:32:38,681
less than the weight of the falling mass,

475
00:32:38,681 --> 00:32:41,974
and more than the weight
of the rising one.

476
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:45,223
That's Newton's three laws of motion.

477
00:32:45,523 --> 00:32:51,499
The first tells us that motion itself
needs no cause: only changes of motion do.

478
00:32:51,799 --> 00:32:55,494
The second tells us how a
net force changes momentum.

479
00:32:55,794 --> 00:33:01,095
And the third tells us that forces
always come from interactions, in pairs.

480
00:33:01,395 --> 00:33:03,356
Master free-body diagrams,

481
00:33:03,356 --> 00:33:07,580
and you can solve a huge range
of problems in mechanics.

482
00:33:07,880 --> 00:33:09,251
Thanks for watching!

